<Base title="Editorial Policy" description="How GuideHollow selects topics, uses official sources and AI assistance, distinguishes examples from testing, and handles corrections." breadcrumbs={[{name:'Editorial Policy',url:'/editorial-policy/'}]}>
<header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow mint">/ editorial_policy</p><h1>Clarity starts<br />with honest limits.</h1><p>Published October 23, 2026. Our standards for useful, accountable AI guides.</p></header><article class="prose info-content"><h2>Our purpose</h2><p>We publish original, practical explanations for everyday AI users. Each guide should solve a recognizable problem, explain the key idea, and give the reader something concrete to try. We prefer a useful small library over a large collection of repetitive articles.</p><h2>Sources and verification</h2><p>We prioritize official product documentation, primary research, and direct technical references. References appear at the end of each article. A source check means we reviewed the linked material; it does not mean every example was executed in every product or account.</p><p>We avoid unsupported price comparisons, model rankings, and performance claims. Features may vary by plan, region, workspace, and product updates. When a detail is uncertain, we qualify it or leave it out. Material without a verifiable factual basis stays unpublished.</p><h2>AI assistance and attribution</h2><p>Our launch content was produced with AI assistance for research organization, drafting, editing, and code implementation. Guides carry the attribution “GuideHollow Editorial.” This attribution describes responsibility for the publication and does not imply a team of named experts or personal product experience.</p><p>Fictional examples are teaching examples. We do not invent interviews, testimonials, benchmarks, credentials, or first-person testing stories. When we report a test, we identify what was actually checked and its limits.</p><h2>Dates and maintenance</h2><p>Publication dates reflect initial preparation for publication. Updated dates change when the content materially changes. References were checked on the date stated in the article. We do not promise continuous monitoring of every third-party document.</p><h2>Corrections</h2><p>Use our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> to flag an error with the affected URL and supporting evidence. Accepted corrections are reflected in the article and public repository history. Significant corrections should explain what changed; silent cosmetic edits do not imply a new fact-check.</p><h2>Commercial independence</h2><p>No paid placement, affiliate links, or advertisements are active at launch. Future sponsorships or affiliate relationships will be disclosed near the relevant content. Advertising must remain distinct from editorial recommendations.</p><h2>Limits of our advice</h2><p>Our guides are educational. They do not replace qualified professional advice for decisions involving health, law, money, safety, or regulated work. Verify important outputs and follow the rules that apply to your organization and situation.</p></article>
</Base>
